Like the opening scroll, it's like, you know, and so they went on, right?

00:53:43.480 --> 00:53:53.860
And the Final Fantasy theme, which is not a franchise theme yet, it's just the theme for this game, but it will appear in every single Final Fantasy game, starts rolling.

00:53:53.860 --> 00:53:57.300
And I'm just like, okay, now this is cool.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:07.320
Like that is early on, and it's, yeah, it's a cool moment in the game, something that is almost saying like, yeah, we know we got a good adventure ahead.

00:54:07.520 --> 00:54:12.540
Like you might have thought that like what we laid down was kind of basic, but wait till you see where this game goes.

00:54:12.700 --> 00:54:18.100
And I think that is the strongest thing about this game is that it continues to go.

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:21.960
It continues to push further as the story progresses.

00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:29.360
It's funny how often that like trope has been used in games up until even now.

00:54:29.620 --> 00:54:37.960
Like you played like a good half hour or even an hour or even two hours of a game, and then you get the title drop.

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:40.860
And I feel like this is the first time we're seeing that.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:43.660
I mean, this is really the first time it could have happened, right?

00:54:44.200 --> 00:54:46.000
Right. I can't think of another example.

00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:50.920
I mean, yeah, how many other games have we played where there's even an hour of gameplay involved?

00:54:53.600 --> 00:54:55.480
Yeah, I love that kind of thing, though.

00:54:55.640 --> 00:54:56.920
I love when I see that in a game.

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:02.460
I think that love started for me with Kingdom Hearts 2 when it's like, yeah, you're like three hours in when the title appears.

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:10.720
But also, I find it very cool that they start this game with like, you need to save the princess.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:12.420
Just like every other game.

00:55:12.700 --> 00:55:14.820
You know, like so many other games have saved the princess.

00:55:15.300 --> 00:55:20.400
And yeah, then when you get to this point, it's like, well, this story is going to evolve.

00:55:20.620 --> 00:55:23.740
And it continues to evolve over and over and over again.

00:55:24.700 --> 00:55:27.000
Like every time you think like, okay, I know what I'm doing.

00:55:27.140 --> 00:55:29.900
I know what this is like. No, there's like a bigger thing happening here that you don't know about.

00:55:30.020 --> 00:55:32.880
And that, I think, is in the DNA of Final Fantasy.

00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:39.880
I think like most, if not all of the Final Fantasy games that I've played, you kind of start out on this like small little journey.

00:55:40.100 --> 00:55:40.980
You feel small.

00:55:41.140 --> 00:55:48.660
But then like as the game goes on, you realize, well, this is a massive world ending, world saving, time loopy thing that like we're doing, you know.

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:51.400
And it's just, I love that about the series.

00:55:51.400 --> 00:55:53.420
And it's cool to see like this is how it started.

00:55:53.820 --> 00:55:56.560
Joe, it's funny that you mentioned that about the princess thing too.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:56:04.060
Because that's even potentially a shot at rival developer Enix who, you know, made Dragon Warrior.

00:56:04.340 --> 00:56:09.060
And in that first game, the whole thing is save the princess, right?

00:56:09.180 --> 00:56:15.140
Like you finally bring her back and you marry her or whatever.

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:21.020
And like that was the game and Final Fantasy is kind of laying that down as like, no, that was just chapter one.

00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:23.740
Like now you got an even bigger journey ahead of you.

00:56:23.880 --> 00:56:35.980
And I think that also says something about how like Dragon Warrior with art by Akira Toriyama of Dragon Ball fame is more like cartoony and silly and friendly.

00:56:36.100 --> 00:56:37.840
You know, the slimes and stuff like that.

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:39.800
Like there's, there's, it's more lighthearted.

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:49.420
Whereas Final Fantasy with Yoshitaka Amano's art, which, you know, he's a much more serious gothic kind of illustrator.

00:56:50.300 --> 00:56:56.780
You know, that art leads into a more, you know, obviously he didn't do the pixel art.

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:03.640
But like the pixel art even itself is darker in tone, those goblins and everything that anything you would have ran into in Dragon Warrior.

00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:18.580
It's more Dungeons and Dragons-esque, which makes total sense when you understand that Final Fantasy, as we know it, the game was basically just ripping from a Dungeons and Dragons bestiary of the time, right?

00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:25.520
Yeah, and that kind of also echoes in the fact that you're creating your party.

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:29.200
Your party doesn't really have any set connection to the world.

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:34.700
I see, I see the, the parallels there.

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:42.880
But also I believe they literally took like exact names and potential stats from the Dungeons and Dragons books.

00:57:42.940 --> 00:57:45.200
Stats? Yeah, into the, into the game.

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:46.380
Oh, wow. Okay.

00:57:46.380 --> 00:57:50.480
Final Fantasy on the NES or sorry, on the Famicom, whatever version.

00:57:50.620 --> 00:57:51.560
I don't mean to single it out.

00:57:51.660 --> 00:57:57.220
But Final Fantasy 1 under the code probably has a lot of explanations for why it's so buggy and broken.

00:57:57.300 --> 00:58:10.380
Because I was surprised at how many calculations and stats are being run in the background through this game where every, every enemy is kind of designed.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:16.640
You know, we're always talking about looking for those curated gameplay experiences and platformers where it's like, yeah, enemies aren't just spawning.

00:58:16.640 --> 00:58:22.100
They were placed there to do this thing when, when you run into that part of the stage.

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:26.480
In a way, Final Fantasy can't do that because of its random encounter system.

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:38.240
But I, I was surprised at like how every time you try to evade, like there is an actual, like beyond a dice roll, there's a calculation of like, well, what's the enemy's evasion score?

00:58:38.360 --> 00:58:39.560
What's your evasion score?

00:58:39.740 --> 00:58:40.560
Roll this number.

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:42.160
What's that number come back as?

00:58:42.360 --> 00:58:45.460
Okay. Then that means that they successfully evade.

00:58:45.600 --> 00:58:55.760
Like there are a lot of stats behind the curtain here that build up like this actual turn-based combat gameplay.

00:58:55.760 --> 00:59:10.680
You think the one that was most visible just to, I guess, the more untrained eye is, uh, the whole concept of absorbed, which I guess is kind of like a defense measurement where it's kind of just like a one-to-one.

00:59:11.140 --> 00:59:16.440
How much, how much HP does your, okay.

00:59:16.500 --> 00:59:20.340
So how much damage do you do and how much of that gets absorbed?

00:59:20.340 --> 00:59:28.880
And that's like, I was just seeing like, I was either doing one damage, two damage, one damage, and then, oh, I, I crit and I finally did 34 damage.

00:59:29.120 --> 00:59:35.220
There's really no way for that to make sense unless there's just like some very basic additive property going on there.

00:59:35.380 --> 00:59:38.720
So it, I was able to at least learn what was going on there.

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:46.480
So if you look at like, uh, I'm just going to pull up the goblin, the very first enemy you'll probably run into in, uh, the NES game.

00:59:46.480 --> 01:00:08.620
Uh, it's common stats are HP 8, Defense 4, Evasion 6, Magic Defense 16, Attack 4, Accuracy 2, Hit Number 1, Crit 1, Experience 6, Gil 6, and then, uh, a, it has, it's immune to undead spells.

01:00:08.620 --> 01:00:19.020
Like, there are other elemental affinities it could possibly have and status affinities it could have, but like, that is a lot of information being programmed for like every single enemy in the game.

01:00:19.740 --> 01:00:25.880
Yeah, it's, it's, it's funny because none of that really means anything to the player, so I guess it doesn't matter that you don't have that.

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:29.020
Of course not, yeah. Is that more than Dragon Warrior?

01:00:29.620 --> 01:00:42.340
Well, see, that I don't know, but I guess what I'm saying is, is that, you know, it, it, behind, like, with the Dungeons and Dragons element of it and everything, that everything is getting, uh, like, rolls and calculated.

01:00:42.840 --> 01:00:45.680
I, I think I just, I think I, that's what, that's the argument I was making.

01:00:45.840 --> 01:00:50.020
Not necessarily that, like, this is the first video game to give defense stats to enemies.

01:00:50.020 --> 01:01:06.460
Oh, okay. Yeah, no, it, it's still impressive, and you can kind of, you can kind of feel, uh, the, the computer, the, the console chugging with how slowly it does take to output those calculations sometimes, but that could also be there for dramatic effect.

01:01:07.760 --> 01:01:28.220
Yeah, but then also it's, it's like a blessing and a curse because it is cool that all that's happening, that makes it so much more robust, but there's a lot of, there's a lot of spells that will up some of those stats or lower stats of an enemy or, you know, like, up your evasion or, or your defense, but again, you don't know what those do.

01:01:28.660 --> 01:01:36.000
And they're all just, like, three letters, so, like, it's cool that it's all in there and it's all part of it, but, like, if I, you know,

01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:37.720
if I didn't have a guide.

01:01:37.840 --> 01:01:49.660
Yeah. Those would be useless, I would never use them, I don't know what, I don't know what B-R-A does, or B-R-A-K does, because it's supposed to be Break, which I still don't know what that does, but, you know, unless I look it up.

01:01:49.720 --> 01:02:05.200
I think the counter-argument to this is that if, if we'd have played it for longer and were super fans, then we'd know and we'd be able to take advantage, but this, this sort of, like, reminds me of playing, like, Nobunaga's Ambition and Romance of the Three Kingdoms, and, like,

01:02:05.200 --> 01:02:11.040
I don't know what this is doing, but it's changing some stat, and I don't know how it's changing that stat, but I'm doing it wrong.

01:02:11.920 --> 01:02:18.380
